            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.149,
            0.877,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Given:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " AFB is an arc. ∠ACB is the angle subtended by arc AFB at centre C. D is a point on the circle outside arc AFB such that when we extend DC it cuts the circle at some point E outside the arc AFB, as shown (Fig. 5.22)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.27,
            0.877,
            0.311
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We shall again make use of the properties of isosceles triangles."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.168,
            0.311,
            0.396,
            0.344
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig 5.22: Angle subtended by arc AFB"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.356,
            0.871,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle ACE = \\angle ADC + \\angle CAD = 2\\angle ADC,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " since "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{CA}=\\mathrm{CD}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (and so "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle ADC = \\angle CAD"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Similarly, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle BCE = \\angle BDC + \\angle CBD = 2\\angle BDC."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Also, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle ACB = \\angle ACE - \\angle BCE,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle ADB = \\angle ADC - \\angle BDC."
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Hence "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle ACB = 2 \\angle ADB."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.464,
            0.472,
            0.692
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.136,
                0.464,
                0.472,
                0.692
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_image_7.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.485,
            0.5,
            0.877,
            0.635
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Something very interesting comes out of this. This is what the activity before Theorem 9 suggested. Take an arc AB. Decide how you want to go from A to B along the arc. Take any point D on the circle, outside arc AB."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.485,
            0.64,
            0.877,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Then, no matter where D is, so long as it is outside the arc and on the circle, ∠ADB is the same!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.18,
            0.697,
            0.421,
            0.73
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 5.23: Angles subtended by an arc are equal"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.736,
            0.886,
            0.865
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us understand this better using Fig. 5.23. Consider the arc of the circle going from A to B via X. The points on the circle outside this arc are points you cross as you go from A to B via E. The angle subtended by AXB at the centre is the angle swept by CA as we move from CA to CB via X. Points E, F and D are all not on AXB. The theorem tells us that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{AEB} = \\angle \\mathrm{ADB} = \\angle \\mathrm{AFB} = \\frac{1}{2} \\angle \\mathrm{ACB}."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
